---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Sidebar from '../components/Sidebar.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import Carousel from '../components/Carousel.astro';
import ContactForm from '../components/ContactForm.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Layout title="Ambotics™" description="A new robotics company. Coming soon.">
  <main id="top" class="min-h-screen w-full">
    <div
      class="mx-auto max-w-[1440px] px-6 sm:px-10 lg:px-[84px] pt-16 sm:pt-20 lg:pt-[104px] pb-24"
    >
      <!-- Single grid: sticky left sidebar (logo + nav) + content -->
      <div class="lg:grid lg:grid-cols-[340px_minmax(0,1fr)]">
        <Sidebar />

        <!-- Content column -->
        <div class="min-w-0 mt-14 lg:mt-0">
          <h1
            class="font-display ss01 max-w-[360px] text-[clamp(28px,5vw,32px)] font-medium leading-[1.05] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink"
          >
            A new kind of robotics company.
          </h1>

          <div class="mt-14 lg:mt-20">
            <!-- The four paragraphs are one continuous argument — ¶3 opens "So the
                 question stops being…" — so the section takes a single heading
                 rather than being split at a seam it does not have. -->
            <section id="starting" class="max-w-content">
              <SectionHeading>Robots are changing</SectionHeading>

              <div
                class="mt-5 space-y-10 body-copy text-[14px] leading-[1.4] tracking-[-0.02em]"
              >
                <p>
                  Robots aren't new. They've been welding and painting in factories
                  since the 1960s and they're very good at it. The catch was always the
                  conditions around them. A conventional robot needs the world held
                  still: parts in fixed positions, a cage, a program written for exactly
                  one task. Change the task and you pay for the integration again.
                  That's why robots filled car plants and stayed rare almost everywhere
                  else.
                </p>
                <p>
                  AI is what's changing them. A robot can now see what's actually in
                  front of it, handle parts that arrive at odd angles, and pick up a new
                  task by being shown it rather than reprogrammed. That moves the line of
                  what's worth automating. Small batches, frequent changeovers and jobs
                  that vary a little every time are all within reach now.
                </p>
                <p>
                  So the question isn't really whether a machine can do the work. It's
                  whether doing it that way pays. That's a business question, and most
                  companies in this field aren't built to answer it — they lead with the
                  hardware and talk to other engineers. We'd rather start with your
                  operation and what the work actually costs you. If automating a task
                  won't produce a real return, we'll tell you and leave it alone.
                </p>
                <p>
                  We build the whole stack ourselves: software, hardware, data, training
                  and evaluation. That means we can change things quickly instead of
                  waiting on a supplier. And when we take a job on, we take all of it —
                  the hardware, the installation, the daily running and the support long
                  after the first day. You shouldn't have to become a robotics expert or
                  line up a row of vendors to keep it working.
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- The section is deliberately not `max-w-content`: the carousel
                 bleeds to the right page edge with a negative margin, so the
                 width cap goes on the prose and the logo wall individually. -->
            <section id="applications" class="mt-24">
              <SectionHeading>Applications</SectionHeading>

              <div
                class="mt-5 max-w-content space-y-10 body-copy text-[14px] leading-[1.4] tracking-[-0.02em]"
              >
                <p>
                  Our team has worked with IKEA, ABB, AstraZeneca and Universal Robots,
                  in retail distribution, industrial robotics and pharmaceutical
                  production. Different operations, but the same question comes up in
                  each. Which repetitive work is worth handing to a machine?
                </p>
                <p>
                  Most conversations start in the same place. Someone suspects a part of
                  their operation could run better but doesn't know what's realistic or
                  where to begin. That's fair enough. This has moved quickly, and
                  there's no easy way to tell from the outside what's possible now.
                </p>
                <p>
                  Tell us where the repetitive work is and we'll give you a straight
                  answer on whether it's worth automating. If it is, we'll build it, run
                  it and stand behind it.
                </p>
              </div>

              <Carousel class="mt-10" />
            </section>

            <ContactForm />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
